   "drive": "Where a car may be (brief 023). The site carries a drive grid (sim/data/handover.site.json `drive`, the walk grid's window and cells, made by tools/sim/drive.ts): a cell is drivable when its centre lies on a carriageway (within half a plan road's width of its centreline), in a kerb bay of the civic block, or on a drive (the world's vehicle crossing from a carriageway over a dropped kerb and the pavement to a bay or yard: civic-block drives[], Splice; the Handover's one is service-drive, Apron Road to serviceBay). Pavements, forecourts, yards and squares are not drivable. Every car that moves on the street routes on drivable cells only, by the walk grid's A* (8 neighbours, no corner cutting, ties by cell index, string-pulled on drivable cells): the turned driver's drive to the service bay with Vale in the back (by the carriageway and the drive, never across a pavement; brief 059), the getaway car's Drive between its stands (cell.driveCar), and the rival car between its points (rulebook rivals). A car's place that lies off the drive grid stands at its nearest drivable cell within 6 m (ties: lowest cell); a place with none, or a car's place that no drive reaches from the pickup, rejects the site at load (unknown = reject). A car keeps off the kerb (brief 023, Keel with Pulse): on a carriageway its centre keeps the site's drive.lane (half the city's lane, life.json traffic.laneWidth) from the edge of the drivable ground where it can, on a drive or in a bay it keeps to the middle; the route never runs nearer an edge than its grid cells did (deterministic, sim Grid.CarRoute). Timing is car.pace's: the drive to the service bay runs at its surfaces' pace from Vale-in (car.pickup), then waits parked in the bay (the driver's camera block is retired, brief 059). Parked in the service bay the car faces the site's car.serviceFacing (the bay's long axis, nose away from the drive it came by). The getaway car leaves the street at Go along its exit lane (getaway.exit, held to the carriageway by SX-1); the rival car drives in, drops three, parks, follows, leaves on a tip and comes back (rulebook rivals). Cars never collide with cars (no rule reads it).",
   "pace": { "road": "v:car-road-cm-s", "yard": "v:car-yard-cm-s" },
-  "footprint": { "len": "v:car-len-cm", "w": "v:car-w-cm", "clear": "v:car-clear-cm", "reach": "v:car-reach-cm", "rule": "A standing car is ground nobody walks through (engine 0.33.1; brief 041, Sky playing 0.2.122: \"characters are walking through cars\"). Every car the mission simulates (car:getaway, car:official, car:rivals; the range's spawned cars) blocks, while it stands, the walk cells whose centres lie within its footprint (`len` along its heading, `w` across, centred on the car's point) grown by `clear` on every side; while it drives it blocks nothing. It stands at a stand or a bay, at its stops, parked, waiting with Vale, stopped where its driver went down or where it was disabled, and as a wreck. Its heading is the one the host draws: the getaway car its stand's facing, the official car its bay's long axis at the pickup (site car.blockFacing) and car.serviceFacing in the service bay, the rival car its stop's facing (rivals.car.facing), a stopped car the way it was driving. Every walk made while it stands goes round it (the walk grid's A* on the free cells it leaves; the grid's own cells never change): a walk that would end on its ground ends at the free cell nearest that point and steps in from there (a person getting into the car), and a walk from inside it (getting out) starts at the free cell nearest. A move order to its ground ends at that free cell (no step in). When a car comes to stand, whoever stands on its ground steps to the free cell nearest, and a walk in hand that would cross its ground is walked again round it, to the same end, from where the walker is (engine 0.36.0, brief 079; an operative mid-act stays, his act holds him). The response unit's walk, made once at load, goes round the mission's start cars. At the car is within `reach` of its body (no longer at-reach-cm of its centre): Board and a rival reaching Vale at the getaway car. A disabled official car's people stand beside it across its heading, the driver on the road side and Vale on the kerb side. The crowd's routine legs go round the cars standing when each leg sets out (read at the start of the tick): the legs built at load go round the mission's start cars (the getaway car at its first stand, the official car at the pickup); once a car stands elsewhere a leg that would cross it is walked again round the cars standing then." },
+  "footprint": { "len": "v:car-len-cm", "w": "v:car-w-cm", "clear": "v:car-clear-cm", "reach": "v:car-reach-cm", "ahead": "v:car-ahead-ticks", "rule": "`ahead` (engine 0.37.0, brief 081): this close to the end of its leg, the rival car's stopping ground is cleared of any agent standing on it. A standing car is ground nobody walks through (engine 0.33.1; brief 041, Sky playing 0.2.122: \"characters are walking through cars\"). Every car the mission simulates (car:getaway, car:official, car:rivals; the range's spawned cars) blocks, while it stands, the walk cells whose centres lie within its footprint (`len` along its heading, `w` across, centred on the car's point) grown by `clear` on every side; while it drives it blocks nothing. It stands at a stand or a bay, at its stops, parked, waiting with Vale, stopped where its driver went down or where it was disabled, and as a wreck. Its heading is the one the host draws: the getaway car its stand's facing, the official car its bay's long axis at the pickup (site car.blockFacing) and car.serviceFacing in the service bay, the rival car its stop's facing (rivals.car.facing), a stopped car the way it was driving. Every walk made while it stands goes round it (the walk grid's A* on the free cells it leaves; the grid's own cells never change): a walk that would end on its ground ends at the free cell nearest that point and steps in from there (a person getting into the car), and a walk from inside it (getting out) starts at the free cell nearest. A move order to its ground ends at that free cell (no step in). When a car comes to stand, whoever stands on its ground steps to the free cell nearest, and a walk in hand that would cross its ground is walked again round it, to the same end, from where the walker is (engine 0.36.0, brief 079; an operative mid-act stays, his act holds him). The response unit's walk, made once at load, goes round the mission's start cars. At the car is within `reach` of its body (no longer at-reach-cm of its centre): Board and a rival reaching Vale at the getaway car. A disabled official car's people stand beside it across its heading, the driver on the road side and Vale on the kerb side. The crowd's routine legs go round the cars standing when each leg sets out (read at the start of the tick): the legs built at load go round the mission's start cars (the getaway car at its first stand, the official car at the pickup); once a car stands elsewhere a leg that would cross it is walked again round the cars standing then." },
   "pickup": { "valeIn": "v:vale-in-s", "rule": "Vale's car at the official bay (brief 059 K1–K4; GDD v0.25.0 §07; Sky's answer 3a). Vale always walks from the official door to his car (5:50 to 6:10) and gets in `valeIn` later. Unturned, his driver drives him away then: the mission fails vale-leaves. Turned, the driver drives him round to the service bay along the drive grid at car.pace (car.parks when it stops) and waits there with Vale in the back until the mission's close (schedule.over), whatever his day in the people file says; at the close, if no Take has begun, he drives Vale on and the mission fails vale-leaves. A Take begun before the close holds him until it ends. With no driver (down) the car waits and Vale stands by it. A Take at his car is in-car (no struggle, nothing to witness) only with the driver turned and the car parked round the back; at the front with a turned driver it is refused (driver-brings-vale), and while the car is driving round (car-not-parked). The driver's Call (the camera block) is retired (Motive M059-6.1)." },
   "paceRule": "A moving car drives each drive-grid cell at its surface's pace: `road` on a carriageway, `yard` on a drive or in a bay. The turned driver leaves the official bay with Vale in the back at Vale-in (car.pickup) and waits parked in the service bay until the close; the getaway car's Drive and the rival car keep the same paces (brief 059).",
