@@ -981,7 +1027,8 @@
   "openRule": "Sky's open questions from brief 059 (decision log 23–25), each a value: S1 is answered (Sky 2026-10-25: no, the final fight always comes): the value fight-always, its rule in alwaysRule (fight.safe.leaveUnfought is retired into it: 1 is the old false and more, 0 the old true); S2 `loud.entry` (nearest, Sky 'yea': the loud fight's officers come from whichever of the response unit's entry (site fight.entry) and the mission's official entrances (the world's entrances of kind official) is the shortest grid walk to the getaway car's stand; response: always the response unit's entry); S3 the value rivals-follow-cm-s (280, run, Sky; the walk is 160).",
   "alwaysRule": "The final fight always comes (Sky's S1; Motive briefs/059/motive/s1-rulings.json M059-S1.2 to S1.5; GDD v0.25.0 §06, §07). With safe.always 1: (1) the gate: from the final fight's start until it is over, Go with Vale aboard is refused (car-held) while the fight's blocker is not out (down or gone). The blocker holds the car wherever he stands while he is up, not only on his lane post. Before a blocker is named, whoever is coming holds it: car:rivals while a member still rides in the rival car (the refusal's tick is the first step-out), a member walking back or due to wake (safe), or the officers on their way (loud). The hold within fight.hold or on the exit lane applies on top as always. Go without Vale (withdrawing by car) and the menu's Withdraw are not gated. (2) The late spring: the ambush springs as built (the Enforcer with Vale, not inside, within safe.ambush of the stand's car) or once Vale is in the car; the blocker is picked at the spring from the crew on foot, and if none is on foot then, at the tick the first one steps out, among those on foot (shortest walk, ties by id); each later arrival alerts as he steps out and fires weapons.aimNew after. (3) The floor (safe.floor): at the safe fight's start, count the members able (alive and not bound: a knocked-out one due to wake counts) and coming (on foot, in the rival car, or due back). If fewer than safe.floor, away members come back, the earliest at the stand first (ties by id), until the count reaches the floor or nobody away is left: the tipped car cuts its tip short, re-enters at rivals.car.entry and rides to the stand's rival stop, where the wheelman steps out `out` s later; the warned lookout walks back from rivals.posts.lookoutExit to his spot (rivals.recalled). Nothing spawns; a killed or bound member stays gone. (4) Crew destroyed: with no member able at the fight's start (all killed or bound, none recalled), the safe fight is over at once (rivals.wiped, fight why crew-destroyed) and the gate is open. 0 turns (1), (3) and (4) off: a cell gone before anyone holds the car leaves unfought (engine 0.32.0).",
   "unguarded": "Hiding in a held car loses (engine 0.34.0; Motive briefs/067/motive.md §2.3, motive-values.json M067-K3, from BAL-YARD-AT-WILL: the Enforcer downed in the car with Vale at 7:30.0 and the run idle to its end; GDD §06: once Vale is in the car the Enforcer is any operative, and hiding in a held car loses). (a) In the safe fight, with Vale in the getaway car, the car held (a holder stands: Motive's amendment, briefs/067/motive-square.md §4: a free car with the cell aboard waits for Go, and the rivals don't walk to it) and no drawn operative standing outside it (engine 0.35.2, Motive briefs/039/motive/rulings-0.35.1.md §2, Sky's yes 2026-10-27: an operative outside guards the car only drawn, gun out or drawing; an undrawn agent standing by is no guard, so a cell that never draws loses here instead of idling to the close), read as each tick starts (after the last tick's orders, so a Go given as the last Board lands is taken first): the rivals up and not broken, the blocker excepted (he keeps his lane), walk to the car, each to the side of it he comes to, firing as they walk and taking no cover or kite step on the way; the first within car.footprint.reach of its body takes Vale (fail rivals-reach-vale, as today: no drawn operative of the cell stands within cell.reach, engine 0.35.2). A drawn operative standing outside again (pressing F answers it), or the car no longer held, sends them back: each takes cover as an advancing rival does (ai.seek.rival, the Enforcer or the car as the foe). Logged rivals.for-car and rivals.back-to-cover. (b) The cell down: the tick the last operative goes down (in the car or not), the mission fails (cell-down), the fail camera on the shot that downed the last to fall. (c) Go needs a driver: Go is taken only with a standing operative in the car; with only the downed aboard it is refused car-no-driver (any standing operative may give it, from anywhere, once one is aboard). No new timer: the safe fight still ends by the cell's acts, (a), (b), Withdraw, or (loud) the seal. The walk is the fight AI's own movement (an advance to a point), not a routine.",
-  "spacing": { "clear": "v:body-clear-cm", "file": "v:body-file-ticks", "rule": "Nobody in a fight stands in anybody else (engine 0.36.0; brief 079, Sky: \"Rivals were overlapping each other\"). Two bodies may pass; where a body's walk ends, or where it stands with none, it claims. The bodies: a final-fight body, a member of the rival crew on foot, the response unit, a police unit, and an operative on the street (the operatives' own moves stay the player's: this rule never moves one). A walk that would end within `clear` of another's claim ends at the free walk cell nearest that place clear of every claim, and outside the car's hold and lane unless the place itself was inside (a blocker's lane point stays his; a walk to a car's door ends at the car). A stand or spot found taken this way gives way to that cell, which is the body's stand from then on. A body that stops mid-walk on another's claim takes the step to such a cell, firing as it walks. Officers sent in together from one entry come in file, `file` apart. A claim is read from the walks and stands already in the state, so nothing new is hashed and the same orders give the same places." },
+  "close": { "warn": "v:close-warn-s", "rule": "The close is a verdict (Motive on Redline E8, brief 080). With Vale in the cell's hands, on foot or in the car, `warn` before the mission's close his absence is noticed: close.warning, once, for the HUD (at once if he is taken later than that). At the close a mission still here fails closed-in, with why: no-driver (nobody aboard can drive; who downed the last of them), car-held (who holds the car), on-foot (Vale still walking; the nearest hostile), no-go (the car free, nobody said go). A Take under way holds the verdict until it ends." },
+  "spacing": { "clear": "v:body-clear-cm", "file": "v:body-file-ticks", "cell": true, "rule": "`cell` (engine 0.37.0, brief 081; proposed, Motive to confirm): an operative's move that would end within `clear` of a claim ends at the free cell nearest it clear of them all, beside the person, not in them. Claims also take the guard, the patrol at rest off its sweep, the waitress and the driver at their places; every response officer spreads the same way as it sets out. Nobody in a fight stands in anybody else (engine 0.36.0; brief 079, Sky: \"Rivals were overlapping each other\"). Two bodies may pass; where a body's walk ends, or where it stands with none, it claims. The bodies: a final-fight body, a member of the rival crew on foot, the response unit, a police unit, and an operative on the street. A place within the getaway car's Board reach stays within it. A walk that would end within `clear` of another's claim ends at the free walk cell nearest that place clear of every claim, and outside the car's hold and lane unless the place itself was inside (a blocker's lane point stays his; a walk to a car's door ends at the car). A stand or spot found taken this way gives way to that cell, which is the body's stand from then on. A body that stops mid-walk on another's claim takes the step to such a cell, firing as it walks. Officers sent in together from one entry come in file, `file` apart. A claim is read from the walks and stands already in the state, so nothing new is hashed and the same orders give the same places." },
   "holdRule": "The hold is entered only on purpose (engine 0.34.0; Motive briefs/067/motive-square.md, motive-values.json M067-SQ1; Pulse's motion): in the final fight a hostile's answer to fire never ends inside the getaway car's hold (`hold` of the car) or on its exit lane: a rival's step out of a shooter's line (the shielding cover, else the nearest free spot within 8 m with no line to him), a rival's step to cover while engaged, a police body's cover or close (fight.kite). If no spot outside serves, it stays where it stands. A walk to a post goes round the hold (the walk grid with the hold's ground closed: the rival crew to its spots, police and the response unit to their stands), unless the post or the walker is already inside it. Exceptions: the blocker (his lane is the road: he comes for the car) and the rivals walking to a held car with nobody outside (fight.unguarded a). An advance on the car or on an operative may enter the hold: that is coming for the car, and it holds. Logged car.held.<how> the first tick each body holds the car at a stand, <how> its reason for being there: blocker, press (for the held car, or the drawn crew for the Vale holder), advance, answer, post (its stand, spot or post, or the way to it); a gate hold by whoever is coming before a blocker is named logs nothing (Waymark's MC-11: no played script logs car.held.answer or car.held.post). Why: only a hostile that comes for the car holds it (MC-11); on the square without its planters the late car's man walked to his ambush spot straight past the car, after the whole cell had boarded a free one, and held it.",
   "hold": "v:fight-hold-m",
   "block": "v:fight-block-m",
@@ -1195,11 +1242,23 @@
